TITLE:: RLTimePoints
summary:: Time points and the pulses between them: the context of metre networks
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLPulse, Classes/RLTimeMap, Classes/RLNetwork, Classes/RLMetre, Classes/RLTimeSpans, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
A time point is a number (a time in bars, in beats, in seconds). The arrows are pulses (link::Classes/RLPulse::): M(d) relates two points a whole number of d apart. A metre network (Popoff and Yust) is an link::Classes/RLNetwork:: on this context: its nodes hold time points, its arrows say which pulse joins them, and two routes compose alike.

Networks are carried into one another (link::Classes/RLNetwork#-isMorphismTo::) in the two ways metre has of being at odds with itself: a pulse included in a finer one (a grouping), and a shift of time (a displacement). The maps of time, link::Classes/RLTimeMap::, are the automorphisms.

code::
// Brahms, "Auf dem See" op. 106 no. 2, bars 7 and 8, the bar as the unit
~time = RLTimePoints.new;
~top = RLNetwork(~time, [[\E, 5/2], [\G, 3]], [[\E, \G, 1/2]]);                              // a half bar
~voice = RLNetwork(~time, [[\E, 5/2], [\F, 17/6], [\G, 3]], [[\E, \F, 1/6], [\F, \G, 1/6]]); // the voice: eighths
~top.isMorphismTo(~voice);                               // true: M1/2 is included in M1/6
~piano = RLNetwork(~time, [[\E, 31/12], [\G, 37/12]], [[\E, \G, 1/2]]);                      // the piano, a sixteenth later
~top.isMorphismTo(~piano, nil, ~time.shift(1/12));       // true: a displacement
~fine = RLNetwork(~time, [[\E, 31/12], [\F, 17/6], [\G, 37/12]], [[\E, \F, 'M1/4'], [\F, \G, 'M1/4']]);   // dotted eighths
~piano.isMorphismTo(~fine);                              // true
~voice.morphismProblems(~fine, nil, ~time.shift(1/12));  // what is in the way of eighths against dotted eighths

~time.dissonance([1/6, 5/2], [1/4, 31/12]);              // grouping
~time.common([1/6, 5/2], [1/4, 31/12]);                  // [0.5, 2.8333333333]: they meet every half bar, at 17/6 first
::

A name with a slash is written between quotes: code::'M1/4'::, not code::\M1/4::, which sclang reads as a division.

Against the article (Popoff and Yust, "Meter networks: a categorical framework for metrical analysis", J. Math. Music, 2020): the relations, their composition, inclusion and intersection, the maps of time and the time points of the song are the article's. Two things differ. A node here holds one time point, where the article's may hold a set of them. And an link::Classes/RLNetwork:: asks by default that two routes between the same nodes compose alike, which the article's networks are not asked: its figure 5 has the beat M1/2 and the eighths M1/6, M1/6 between the same two points. That figure is a free network (link::Classes/RLNetwork#-free::):
code::
// the article's time points: the second half of bar 7 at 0
~fig5 = RLNetwork(~time, [[\A2, 0], [\A3, 1/3], [\A8, 1/2], [\C2, 1/12], [\C3, 1/3], [\C8, 7/12]], [
	[\A2, \A3, 1/6], [\A3, \A8, 1/6], [\A2, \A8, 1/6], [\A2, \A8, 1/2],
	[\C2, \C3, 1/4], [\C3, \C8, 1/4], [\C2, \C8, 1/4], [\C2, \C8, 1/2],
	[\A2, \C2, 1/12], [\A3, \C3, 0], [\A8, \C8, 1/12]]).free;
~fig5.isValid;                                           // true (false when read strictly)

// its morphisms carry a relation at each node (M0: the point does not move). A shift by u without a map of
// time: M(u) at each node
~half = RLNetwork(~time, [[\A2, 0], [\A8, 1/2]], [[\A2, \A8, 1/2]]);
~later = RLNetwork(~time, [[\B2, 1/12], [\B8, 7/12]], [[\B2, \B8, 1/2]]);
~half.isMorphismTo(~later, [[\A2, \B2], [\A8, \B8]], ~time.shift(1/12));    // true
~half.isMorphismTo(~later, [[\A2, \B2], [\A8, \B8]], nil, 1/12);            // true
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: arrow
A pulse from what names it: an link::Classes/RLPulse::, a period, a name (code::'M1/6'::, code::"1/6"::), or a word of them (their gcd).

METHOD:: transform
METHOD:: pulse
The same, for one name or one period.

METHOD:: identityTransform
M0.

METHOD:: nameOf
A pulse's name.

METHOD:: affine
METHOD:: shift
METHOD:: stretch
The maps of time code::t -> a t + b:: (link::Classes/RLTimeMap::): both, a shift alone, a stretch alone.

METHOD:: pulseOf
The finest pulse that holds a list of time points: every two of them a whole number of its periods apart.

METHOD:: layer
The points of a pulse layer in a window. A layer is code::[period, phase]::, or a period alone: the points phase + k period. A layer of period 0 is its one point.

METHOD:: dissonance
How two pulse layers stand to one another: code::\consonance:: when the points of the slower are all points of the faster; code::\displacement:: when their periods would allow that and they are shifted apart (Krebs); code::\grouping:: when neither period is a whole multiple of the other. One point alone (period 0) is the slowest of layers: on the other layer (a consonance) or off it.

METHOD:: common
The points two layers both sound, as a layer code::[period, its first point from the first layer's phase on]::: the period is their lcm. nil when they share no point.

METHOD:: system
nil: time points have no pitch system.
